---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { servicePricingFaqs } from '../../data/pricing';
import PageIntro from '../../components/PageIntro.astro';
import { sitePhone } from '../../data/branding';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Restaurant SEO Agency Toronto | Toronto Restaurant Growth"
  description="Restaurant SEO agency for independent restaurants in Toronto and the GTA. Business Profile, citations, website SEO, reviews, and monthly reporting."
>

  <main>

    <PageIntro
      eyebrow="Restaurant SEO"
      title="Restaurant SEO Agency for Toronto Restaurants"
      description="Toronto Restaurant Growth works only with independent restaurants. Restaurant SEO here means the Google Business Profile, directory listings, website pages, and reviews that help nearby diners find a specific room in Toronto or the GTA."
      ctaLabel="Book a Free Google Audit"
      ctaHref="/contact/"
    />

    <section class="bg-white py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-24"><div class="trg-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2>What restaurant SEO includes</h2>
        <p>
          A restaurant SEO agency is not a general marketing retainer with a food photo attached. The work is the set of pages and listings a diner actually uses: Google Maps, the website, and the reviews next to the name. Growth covers that stack. Social video is a separate add-on, described on the <a href="/services/social-media-management/">social media page</a> and on <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Google Business Profile</h3>
        <p>
          Most owners claim the profile and stop at the name, address, and phone. We set the primary and secondary categories, attributes, photos, Q&amp;A, and a posting rhythm so the listing stays current.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Category optimization</strong> — primary and secondary categories set for the searches you want to be eligible for</li>
          <li><strong>Weekly Google Posts</strong> — specials, events, and promotions posted on the Maps listing</li>
          <li><strong>Photo uploads</strong> — fresh photography on the profile so it does not look abandoned</li>
          <li><strong>Q&amp;A management</strong> — common customer questions answered on the listing</li>
          <li><strong>Attributes</strong> — halal, outdoor seating, parking, accessibility, and the other filters diners use</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Citations and a consistent name, address, and phone</h3>
        <p>
          We build and correct citations on the directories diners and Google both see: Yelp, TripAdvisor, OpenTable, Facebook, Apple Maps, Bing Places, and smaller food directories. The name, address, and phone on those listings should match the website and the Google profile. The <a href="/resources/restaurant-seo-checklist/">restaurant SEO checklist</a> is the same list, in a form you can walk through yourself.
        </p>

        <h3>Website pages that support the listing</h3>
        <p>
          Maps visibility is tied to the website. The site needs the same name, address, and phone, a menu in HTML rather than only a PDF, and pages that name the neighbourhood and the food you actually serve. That build is the <a href="/services/restaurant-website-design/">restaurant website design</a> service. Location pages for the GTA cities we already cover are listed under <a href="/locations/">locations</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Reviews</h3>
        <p>
          Review count, rating, and how recently new reviews arrive all show up next to the listing. Generating reviews and writing the responses is <a href="/services/restaurant-review-management/">restaurant review management</a>, included in the same Growth plan as the SEO work.
        </p>

        <h3>Monthly reporting</h3>
        <p>
          Each month you get the keywords we are watching, Business Profile views, calls, direction requests, and how nearby competitors' listings compare. The report is a record of what changed. It is not a promise of a rank.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-surface py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-24"><div class="trg-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2>How the restaurant SEO process works</h2>
        <p>
          The sequence is the same for a room in Toronto, Scarborough, Brampton, Mississauga, or another GTA city. The profile and the website come before new creative.
        </p>

        <h3>1. Free Google audit</h3>
        <p>
          We review the Business Profile, the website, and the reviews, then tell you what is incomplete. The audit is the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>. It is 15 minutes and it is free. You can also call <a href={`tel:${sitePhone.tel}`}>{sitePhone.display}</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Profile and listings</h3>
        <p>
          Categories, hours, menu links, photos, and duplicate listings come first. Citations are corrected to one version of the name, address, and phone. This is the unglamorous part, and it is the part that makes later content useful.
        </p>

        <h3>3. On-site pages</h3>
        <p>
          We add or revise the pages Google can read: menu text, neighbourhood context, and a contact page that matches the profile. If a page cannot be written honestly for that neighbourhood, we do not publish a thin one. The reasoning is in <a href="/blog/why-your-restaurant-not-showing-on-google/">why a restaurant does not show on Google</a> and <a href="/blog/toronto-restaurants-rank-google-maps/">how Toronto restaurants show up on Google Maps</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Reviews and the monthly report</h3>
        <p>
          After the profile and the site agree with each other, review requests and public replies become a routine, and the monthly report shows what moved. Competitive terms take longer than neighbourhood terms. We say that at the start, using your keywords, rather than quoting a single timeline for every restaurant.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-24"><div class="trg-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2>Google Maps SEO is part of restaurant SEO</h2>
        <p>
          For restaurants, the Map pack is usually the result that gets the call or the direction request. Organic pages still matter, because they support that listing and they catch searches that do not show a map.
        </p>

        <div class="trg-stat-grid">
          <div class="trg-stat-card">
            <div class="value">86%</div>
            <div class="label">of people use Google Maps to find restaurants near them</div>
          </div>
          <div class="trg-stat-card">
            <div class="value">70%</div>
            <div class="label">of clicks on Google Maps searches go to the top 3 results</div>
          </div>
          <div class="trg-stat-card">
            <div class="value">$0</div>
            <div class="label">ad spend required — organic Maps ranking is free traffic forever</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>An incomplete profile</h3>
        <p>
          Google has more to go on when the profile is filled in: categories, attributes, photos, Q&amp;A, and posts. An empty profile is a weak relevance signal next to a competitor who updates theirs.
        </p>

        <h3>Review velocity</h3>
        <p>
          A restaurant with 200 reviews and none in the last 60 days can lose ground to a competitor collecting new reviews every month. Recency matters along with the total.
        </p>

        <h3>The website under the listing</h3>
        <p>
          If the site has no neighbourhood content, no structured data, or a name and phone that do not match the profile, the listing has less support. <a href="/services/">All five services</a> are built for restaurants; this page is the search piece.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-surface py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-24"><div class="trg-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2>Work already published</h2>
        <p>
          Two write-ups are on the site. Read them for the scope of the work. They are not a forecast for a new client.
        </p>
        <div style="border-left: 4px solid #E85D04; padding: 32px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 24px 0; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);">
          <p>Position 8 to Position 2 in 6 months</p>
          <p>After implementing our full Google Maps SEO stack for Crab Boil — GBP optimization, review generation, local citations, and weekly posting — their ranking for "seafood restaurant Toronto" jumped from position 8 to position 2. Combined with 280+ reviews, their GBP drives over 1,200 direction requests per month.</p>
          <p><a href="/case-studies/crab-boil/">Read the full Crab Boil case study</a></p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Mystic Caribbean's write-up is the other public report: <a href="/case-studies/mystic-caribbean/">Mystic Caribbean on Kennedy Road</a>. The <a href="/case-studies/">case studies index</a> lists both.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-surface py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-24"><div class="trg-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>What does a restaurant SEO agency do?</h3>
          <p>For an independent restaurant, the work is the Google Business Profile, citations, website pages, and reviews. Toronto Restaurant Growth does that only for restaurants in Toronto and the GTA. The included list is above. Fees are on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>Is restaurant SEO different from Google Maps SEO?</h3>
          <p>Maps is the local pack: proximity, the Business Profile, reviews, and citations. Restaurant SEO also includes the website pages that should match that profile and rank for searches that do not show a map. We do both together because they support each other. We do not guarantee a position or a date.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>How much does Google Maps SEO cost for a restaurant in Toronto?</h3>
          <p style="color: #4a4a4a;" set:html={servicePricingFaqs.googleMaps} />
          <p>Most clients see meaningful ranking improvements within 60–90 days.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>How long does it take to rank higher on Google Maps?</h3>
          <p>Initial ranking improvements typically appear within 30–60 days for less competitive searches and neighbourhoods. Competitive search terms like "best restaurant downtown Toronto" take 3–6 months of sustained optimization. We'll be honest with you about the timeline for your specific market and keywords at the outset.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>Do I need a website for Google Maps SEO to work?</h3>
          <p>You don't strictly need one, but a strong website significantly amplifies your Maps ranking. We recommend building them together — the website's search signals support the Maps ranking, and the Maps listing drives traffic to the website. Standalone GBP optimization without a website will plateau faster.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>What are restaurant SEO services for Toronto restaurants?</h3>
          <p>Restaurant SEO services for Toronto restaurants combine Google Business Profile optimization, local citations, review velocity, and location-specific website content — the signals Google uses to decide who appears in the Maps pack. For Mystic Caribbean in Brampton, that stack moved them to #1 on Kennedy Road with 15 keywords in the top 10. <a href="/case-studies/mystic-caribbean/">See the Mystic Caribbean case study</a> for the full breakdown.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="trg-faq-item">
          <h3>Do you offer Google Maps ranking services in Toronto?</h3>
          <p>Yes — we offer Google Maps ranking services in Toronto and across the GTA, from GBP setup through ongoing rank tracking and reporting. Our work with Mystic Caribbean shows what's possible when Maps, reviews, and on-site SEO work together: dominant local visibility with a live dashboard that proves ROI. <a href="/case-studies/mystic-caribbean/">Read the Mystic Caribbean case study</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-surface py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-24"><div class="trg-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 text-center sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2>See what your listing is missing</h2>
        <p>
          The audit covers the Google Business Profile, the website, and the reviews. We will point at specific gaps. We will not invent a rank you have not earned.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/contact/">Book a Free Google Audit</a>
          <span> · </span>
          <a href={`tel:${sitePhone.tel}`}>Call {sitePhone.display}</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>
